	"nodes":[
		{"id":"instructions","type":"text","text":"**Start Here:**\n1. Drag an arrow from the Abstract (or any future answer) to a new card.\n2. Write your question.\n3. Tell the CLI: \"canvas reply\"\n\nI will trace the path backwards to maintain non-linear context!","x":160,"y":-200,"width":400,"height":200,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"6c2b4dbc047e3ada","type":"text","text":"# Question 1\nWhat is a modern approach to calculating intraband and interband transitions properly? Specifically emission and emission under illumination - photoluminescence.","x":-355,"y":200,"width":335,"height":220},
		{"id":"context_abstract","type":"text","text":"# Context: Metal Photoluminescence\n\n**Summary:**\nThis work quantifies the contribution of **interband transitions** (conduction to valence band) to the photoluminescence of Gold under external illumination. We extend previous theoretical frameworks—originally limited to intraband transitions—to include interband channels under both continuous-wave and transient excitation. Our approach combines rigorous numerical simulations with analytical validations.","x":-642,"y":-200,"width":660,"height":280},
		{"id":"bb8e04d7c240d195","type":"text","text":"# Answer 1 \\u2014 Modern workflow (intra + inter, absorption + PL)\\n\\nA clean modern way to do this is to compute two objects built from the same bandstructure + matrix elements:\\n\\n- **Linear response / absorption:** the retarded correlator $$\\\\Pi^{R}(\\\\omega)$$ or conductivity $$\\\\sigma(\\\\omega)$$.\\n- **Spontaneous emission / PL:** the lesser correlator $$\\\\Pi^{<}(\\\\omega)$$ (non-equilibrium current fluctuations).\\n\\nIn equilibrium, $$\\\\Pi^{<}$$ and $$\\\\Pi^{R}$$ are related by fluctuation\\u2013dissipation, so Kirchhoff\\u2019s law drops out automatically ([[A9 - Kirchhoff's Law Of Radiation]]). Under illumination you must compute $$\\\\Pi^{<}$$ from the pumped occupations.\\n\\n## 1) Inputs (band + matrix elements)\\n\\nPick a model giving Bloch energies $$\\\\varepsilon_{n\\\\mathbf{k}}$$ and optical matrix elements. In a band basis define\\n\\n$$v^{\\\\alpha}_{nm}(\\\\mathbf{k})\\\\equiv \\\\frac{1}{\\\\hbar}\\\\langle n\\\\mathbf{k}\\\\rvert \\\\partial_{k_{\\\\alpha}}H_{0}(\\\\mathbf{k})\\\\lvert m\\\\mathbf{k}\\\\rangle.$$\\n\\nThese $$v_{nm}$$ (or equivalent dipoles) are the only \\u2018microscopic\\u2019 ingredients in the standard independent-particle formulas.\\n\\n## 2) Absorption (and the intra/inter split)\\n\\nDefine the Kubo conductivity via the retarded current correlator,\\n\\n$$\\\\sigma_{\\\\alpha\\\\beta}(\\\\omega)=\\\\frac{1}{i\\\\omega}\\\\Big(\\\\Pi^{R}_{\\\\alpha\\\\beta}(\\\\omega)-\\\\Pi^{R}_{\\\\alpha\\\\beta}(0)\\\\Big).$$\\n\\nIn the independent-particle (Kubo\\u2013Greenwood) approximation,\\n\\n$$\\\\Re\\\\,\\\\sigma_{\\\\alpha\\\\beta}(\\\\omega)=\\\\frac{\\\\pi e^{2}}{\\\\hbar V}\\\\sum_{\\\\mathbf{k}}\\\\sum_{n,m}(f_{n\\\\mathbf{k}}-f_{m\\\\mathbf{k}})\\\\,v^{\\\\alpha}_{nm}(\\\\mathbf{k})v^{\\\\beta}_{mn}(\\\\mathbf{k})\\\\,\\\\delta(\\\\varepsilon_{m\\\\mathbf{k}}-\\\\varepsilon_{n\\\\mathbf{k}}-\\\\hbar\\\\omega).$$\\n\\n- **Interband:** $$n\\\\neq m$$, delta broadened by $$\\\\eta\\\\sim \\\\hbar/(2\\\\tau_{\\\\mathrm{inter}})$$.\\n- **Intraband:** $$n=m$$ becomes finite only with momentum relaxation. In an RTA/Drude form,\\n\\n$$\\\\sigma^{\\\\mathrm{intra}}_{\\\\alpha\\\\beta}(\\\\omega)=\\\\frac{e^{2}}{V}\\\\sum_{n\\\\mathbf{k}}\\\\Big(-\\\\frac{\\\\partial f}{\\\\partial\\\\varepsilon}\\\\Big)_{\\\\varepsilon_{n\\\\mathbf{k}}}\\\\,v_{n\\\\mathbf{k},\\\\alpha}v_{n\\\\mathbf{k},\\\\beta}\\\\,\\\\frac{\\\\tau_{n\\\\mathbf{k}}}{1-i\\\\omega\\\\tau_{n\\\\mathbf{k}}}.$$\\n\\nFrom $$\\sigma(\\\\omega)=\\\\sigma^{\\\\mathrm{intra}}+\\\\sigma^{\\\\mathrm{inter}}$$ you get $$\\\\epsilon(\\\\omega)=\\\\epsilon_{\\\\infty}+i\\\\sigma/(\\\\epsilon_{0}\\\\omega)$$ and thus absorptivity $$A(\\\\omega)$$ for your geometry.\\n\\n## 3) Emission at equilibrium (thermal)\\n\\nEquilibrium emission is controlled by fluctuation\\u2013dissipation:\\n\\n$$\\\\Pi^{<}(\\\\omega)= -2i\\\\,n_{B}(\\\\omega,T)\\\\,\\\\Im\\\\,\\\\Pi^{R}(\\\\omega),\\\\quad n_{B}=\\\\frac{1}{e^{\\\\hbar\\\\omega/k_{B}T}-1}.$$\\n\\nSince $$\\\\Im\\\\,\\\\Pi^{R}(\\\\omega)=\\\\omega\\\\,\\\\Re\\\\,\\\\sigma(\\\\omega)$$, emission is a Bose factor times dissipation. Macroscopically this is Kirchhoff:\\n\\n$$\\\\mathcal{E}(\\\\omega,T)=A(\\\\omega)\\\\,u_{\\\\mathrm{BB}}(\\\\omega,T).$$\\n\\nSo for equilibrium emission, the \\u2018proper\\u2019 modern route is: compute $$\\\\sigma(\\\\omega)$$ accurately (intra+inter) and do optics.\\n\\n## 4) Photoluminescence under illumination (non-equilibrium)\\n\\nUnder pumping, detailed balance fails so Kirchhoff/FDT cannot determine $$\\\\Pi^{<}$$. In a weak-coupling independent-particle picture you can write an explicit band sum (same ingredients as Kubo\\u2013Greenwood, but with emission occupations):\\n\\n$$\\\\Pi^{<}_{\\\\alpha\\\\beta}(\\\\omega)=\\\\frac{2\\\\pi i e^{2}}{\\\\hbar V}\\\\sum_{\\\\mathbf{k}}\\\\sum_{n,m} f_{n\\\\mathbf{k}}(1-f_{m\\\\mathbf{k}})\\\\,v^{\\\\alpha}_{nm}(\\\\mathbf{k})v^{\\\\beta}_{mn}(\\\\mathbf{k})\\\\,\\\\delta(\\\\varepsilon_{m\\\\mathbf{k}}-\\\\varepsilon_{n\\\\mathbf{k}}-\\\\hbar\\\\omega).$$\\n\\nAll the \\u2018physics under illumination\\u2019 is in $$f_{n\\\\mathbf{k}}$$. Compute it from a kinetic equation ([[A6 - Boltzmann Equation]]):\\n\\n$$\\\\partial_{t}f = (\\\\partial_{t}f)_{\\\\mathrm{pump}}+(\\\\partial_{t}f)_{e-e}+(\\\\partial_{t}f)_{e-ph}+\\\\cdots.$$\\n\\nTwo common metal approximations:\\n\\n- **Hot FD:** $$f_{n\\\\mathbf{k}}\\\\approx [e^{(\\\\varepsilon_{n\\\\mathbf{k}}-\\\\mu(T_{e}))/k_{B}T_{e}}+1]^{-1}$$.\\n- **FD + correction:** $$f=f^{T_{e}}+\\\\delta f$$ from an RTA pump\\u2013relax balance (your structure in [[A6 - Boltzmann Equation]]).\\n\\nPlug this $$f$$ into the same emission integral structure you already use in [[2.1 - General Emission Integral]].\\n\\n## 5) Intraband emission subtlety (why scattering matters)\\n\\nDirect interband PL can be treated as vertical $$\\\\mathbf{k}_{f}\\\\approx\\\\mathbf{k}_{i}$$ transitions (your [[A8 - Interband Transitions (new)]] derivation). For intraband emission in an ideal crystal, photon momentum is too small: with $$\\\\mathbf{k}_{f}\\\\approx\\\\mathbf{k}_{i}$$ you cannot satisfy $$\\\\varepsilon_{n\\\\mathbf{k}_{f}}-\\\\varepsilon_{n\\\\mathbf{k}_{i}}=\\\\hbar\\\\omega\\\\neq 0$$. Thus intraband PL is **scattering-assisted** (phonons, impurities, boundaries), and is equivalently captured by:\\n\\n- a Drude/RTA treatment (encode momentum relaxation in $$\\\\tau$$), or\\n- an explicit second-order \\u2018photon + scatterer\\u2019 golden-rule calculation (microscopic justification of Drude).\\n\\n## 6) Practical numerical checklist\\n\\n- Converge k-grids and broadening (see [[3.1 - Numeric Convergence]]).\\n- Validate absorption with limits and the f-sum rule $$\\\\int_{0}^{\\\\infty}d\\\\omega\\\\,\\\\Re\\\\sigma(\\\\omega)=\\\\pi ne^{2}/(2m)$$.\\n- For PL, verify that if you set $$f$$ to an equilibrium FD distribution you recover the equilibrium Bose factor relation (consistency with [[A9 - Kirchhoff's Law Of Radiation]]).\\n- Convert the microscopic emission to what a detector sees using an optical outcoupling factor (Fresnel transmission / LDOS if nanostructured).","x":200,"y":200,"width":720,"height":1100}
	],
